---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'PageSpeed Optimization Libraries (PSOL)',
  description:
    "PSOL was Google's server-independent C++ optimization core inside mod_pagespeed. The original project's last open-source release shipped in 2020. The same engine is maintained today and runs in your web server, in front of any origin, or inside ASP.NET Core.",
  about: 'PageSpeed Optimization Libraries',
  image: 'https://modpagespeed.com/og-default.png?v=2',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'We-Amp B.V.' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'We-Amp B.V.',
    logo: {
      '@type': 'ImageObject',
      url: 'https://modpagespeed.com/logo.svg',
    },
  },
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://modpagespeed.com/psol/',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="PageSpeed Optimization Libraries (PSOL) — mod_pagespeed 2.1"
  description="PSOL was Google's C++ optimization core inside mod_pagespeed. Its last open-source release shipped in 2020. The engine is maintained — run it today."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <h1 class="h1-hero text-text-body">PageSpeed Optimization Libraries (PSOL)</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-text-muted">
      PSOL was Google's server-independent C++ optimization engine &mdash; the core inside
      mod_pagespeed that rewrote HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and images. The original project's last
      open-source release shipped in 2020, under the Apache incubator. The engine kept working, and
      it's still maintained. Here's how to run it today.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-10">
      <!-- What PSOL was -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">What PSOL was</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          A set of C++ classes that optimized web pages and the resources they load, independent of
          any one web server. The same core powered mod_pagespeed on Apache and ngx_pagespeed on
          nginx. It ran on Linux, and building it meant building mod_pagespeed first.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- What happened to it -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">What happened to it</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          Google open-sourced mod_pagespeed in 2010. Its last Google release was 1.13.35.2 in 2018;
          the final open-source release came in 2020 under the Apache incubator. Development
          continued at
          <a
            href="https://we-amp.com/"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline">We-Amp B.V.</a
          >, the team that helped build ngx_pagespeed, maintained mod_pagespeed, and drove the
          project's Apache incubation. The optimization algorithms PSOL proved across billions of
          pages still ship today &mdash; patched for CVEs, built on modern toolchains, and
          supported.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Run the engine today -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Run the optimization engine today</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          PSOL is no longer a standalone build target, but the optimizations it pioneered are
          unchanged &mdash; <a
            href="/how-it-works/"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
            >how the engine rewrites a page</a
          > works the way it always has. The same core now ships three ways. Pick the one that matches
          where your HTML lives.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-1">
          <!-- module -->
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-secondary p-6">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-body">
              In your web server &mdash; the module
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-text-muted">
              A drop-in module for Apache, nginx, and IIS. Your existing configuration and the <a
                href="/features/"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
                >classic filter set</a
              > &mdash; combine, inline, minify, image rewriting &mdash; keep working.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-text-muted">
              <a
                href="/"
                class="font-medium text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
                >mod_pagespeed 2.1 overview &rarr;</a
              >
              <span class="mx-2 text-stone-400">&middot;</span>
              <a
                href="/docs/"
                class="font-medium text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
                >Docs &rarr;</a
              >
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- worker -->
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-secondary p-6">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-body">
              In front of any origin &mdash; the optimizer worker
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-text-muted">
              A separate worker process optimizes any HTTP origin and is driven by an HTTP API.
              Nothing to compile into your server.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-text-muted">
              <a
                href="/docs/"
                class="font-medium text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
                >Read the docs &rarr;</a
              >
              <span class="mx-2 text-stone-400">&middot;</span>
              <a
                href="/docs/http-api/"
                class="font-medium text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
                >Worker HTTP API &rarr;</a
              >
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- NuGet -->
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-secondary p-6">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-body">
              Inside ASP.NET Core &mdash; WeAmp.PageSpeed.AspNetCore
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-text-muted">
              A NuGet package runs the native C/C++ core inside your .NET request pipeline via
              P/Invoke. No reverse proxy, no sidecar.
            </p>
            <a
              href="/docs/aspnet-getting-started/"
              class="mt-3 inline-block font-medium text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >Install via NuGet &rarr;</a
            >
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Old API docs -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Where the old PSOL API docs went</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          The Doxygen class reference that lived at <code
            class="rounded bg-bg-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm">/psol/hierarchy.html</code
          > is gone, and we don't publish PSOL as a standalone library to link against. If you came here
          for programmatic access to the optimization engine, the optimizer worker HTTP API is the maintained
          equivalent &mdash; every endpoint the web console uses is open to your own scripts.
        </p>
        <a
          href="/docs/http-api/"
          class="mt-3 inline-block font-medium text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >Worker HTTP API &rarr;</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
